HUB.add({
  id: 'compare', title: 'Comparisons: X vs Y and when to use', short: 'X vs Y',
  blurb: 'The "difference between" and "when should you use" questions, in pairs.',
  items: [
{ q: `What is the difference between React and ReactDOM, and when should you use each?`,
  k: `React is the brain that decides what the picture looks like; ReactDOM is the hand that paints it on the web page.`,
  a: `<code>react</code> contains the core: components, hooks, elements, reconciliation-agnostic APIs. <code>react-dom</code> is the renderer for browsers: <code>createRoot</code>, <code>hydrateRoot</code>, <code>createPortal</code>, <code>flushSync</code>, form hooks like <code>useFormStatus</code>, and <code>react-dom/server</code> for SSR. You use React everywhere; ReactDOM only at the web entry point and for DOM-specific APIs. React Native uses a different renderer with the same <code>react</code> package.` },
{ q: `How do you use ReactDOM to render a React component into the DOM?`,
  k: `Pick a spot on the wall and tell the painter to start there.`,
  a: `Create a root on a container element and render. Hydrate instead when the HTML came from the server.`,
  c: `import { createRoot } from 'react-dom/client'
const root = createRoot(document.getElementById('root'))
root.render(<StrictMode><App /></StrictMode>)
// later: root.unmount()`,
  u: `ReactDOM.render was removed in React 19.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between class components and functional components in React?`,
  k: `Class bricks are old robots with a body called "this"; function bricks are recipes with magic stickers.`,
  a: `<b>Class</b>: <code>this.state</code>/<code>setState</code>, lifecycle methods, <code>this</code> binding, logic reuse via HOCs/render props, can be error boundaries. <b>Function</b>: hooks for state and effects, less code, logic reuse via custom hooks, easier to test, work with the React Compiler, Server Components and new React 19 APIs. Functions capture props per render (closures), classes read the latest <code>this.props</code>.` },
{ q: `When should you use class components over functional components, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Use recipe bricks for everything new; keep old robots only where they still live.`,
  a: `Use function components for all new code. Keep class components only in legacy code you aren't refactoring yet, and for error boundaries (or use <code>react-error-boundary</code>). Migrate classes gradually when you touch them.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between useState and useReducer hooks in React?`,
  k: `useState: you change the box yourself. useReducer: you send a message and the rule book changes the box.`,
  a: `<code>useState</code> stores a value and gives a setter, simple for independent values. <code>useReducer</code> centralises update logic in a pure reducer that responds to actions, better for related fields and complex transitions, and testable outside React. <code>useState</code> is implemented on top of a reducer internally.` },
{ q: `When should you use useState instead of useReducer, and vice versa?`,
  k: `One or two simple boxes: useState. A box with lots of rules: useReducer.`,
  a: `useState for a few independent primitives (open/closed, input text). useReducer when the next state depends on the previous in several ways, many fields update together, actions describe user intent (add, remove, reset), or you want to pass a stable <code>dispatch</code> deep down with context.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between useEffect and useLayoutEffect hooks in React?`,
  k: `useLayoutEffect works before the picture is shown; useEffect works right after.`,
  a: `<code>useLayoutEffect</code> runs synchronously after DOM mutations but <b>before the browser paints</b>, blocking paint. <code>useEffect</code> runs after paint (non-blocking). Both have the same signature and cleanup. <code>useLayoutEffect</code> does nothing on the server (warns in older versions).` },
{ q: `When should you use useEffect over useLayoutEffect, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Almost always useEffect. Use the "before" one only to measure and fix the picture so it doesn't wobble.`,
  a: `Default to <code>useEffect</code> (subscriptions, fetching, logging). Use <code>useLayoutEffect</code> only when you must read layout and synchronously change the DOM to avoid a visible flicker: tooltip positioning, measuring size before showing, scroll restoration. <code>useInsertionEffect</code> is only for CSS-in-JS libraries.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between useMemo and useCallback hooks in React?`,
  k: `useMemo remembers an answer; useCallback remembers an instruction card.`,
  a: `<code>useMemo(fn, deps)</code> calls <code>fn</code> and caches its <b>result</b>. <code>useCallback(fn, deps)</code> caches the <b>function itself</b>. <code>useCallback(fn, deps) === useMemo(() =&gt; fn, deps)</code>.` },
{ q: `When should you use useMemo instead of useCallback, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Remember an answer if it's hard to work out; remember a card if a picky child checks if the card changed.`,
  a: `useMemo for expensive computations or to keep an object/array reference stable. useCallback when passing a callback to a memoized child or using it as an effect/hook dependency. If neither identity nor cost matters, use neither. With the React Compiler, mostly unnecessary.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between Context API and Redux for state management in React?`,
  k: `Context is a loudspeaker; Redux is a whole library with a librarian, rules and a replay camera.`,
  a: `<b>Context</b> is a dependency-injection mechanism: it passes a value down, every consumer re-renders when it changes, no built-in update logic, devtools or middleware. <b>Redux</b> is a state management library: central store, actions/reducers, selector-based subscriptions (only affected components re-render), middleware, DevTools, RTK Query. Context + useReducer can mimic Redux for small apps.` },
{ q: `When should you use Context API over Redux, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Loudspeaker for a few house rules; librarian for a big, busy house.`,
  a: `Context for low-frequency global values (theme, locale, auth user) or to pass dependencies. Redux (or Zustand) when many components read and update shared state often, you need predictable traced updates, middleware, or a large team with conventions. Server data: neither, use a query cache.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between React and React Native?`,
  k: `Same Lego brain; one builds websites, the other builds phone apps.`,
  a: `Both use React components and hooks. React (with react-dom) renders HTML elements in browsers and styles with CSS. React Native renders native mobile views (<code>View</code>, <code>Text</code>, <code>Pressable</code>) using a JavaScript-subset of styles (Flexbox) and native modules; the New Architecture (Fabric, TurboModules, JSI) is now default. Navigation, storage and platform APIs differ. Expo is the recommended way to start.` },
{ q: `When should you use React Native instead of React, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Building for the app store? React Native. Building a website? React.`,
  a: `React Native for iOS/Android apps that need native UI, device APIs, offline and app-store distribution with one codebase. React for web apps, SEO-heavy sites, and when a responsive web app or PWA is enough. Share logic (hooks, API clients, state) across both in a monorepo.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between Next.js and Create React App for building React applications?`,
  k: `CRA gave you an empty toy box; Next.js gives you a whole playground with paths and a kitchen.`,
  a: `CRA was a client-only SPA starter (Webpack, no routing, no SSR) and is <b>deprecated</b> (Feb 2025). Next.js is a full framework: file-based routing, Server Components, SSR, SSG/ISR, streaming, image and font optimisation, API routes, middleware, and deployment integrations.`,
  u: `Compare Next.js with Vite today: Vite = fast client-side SPA tooling; Next.js = full-stack React framework.` },
{ q: `When should you use Next.js over Create React App, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Need a playground with a kitchen? Next.js. Just a small toy box? Vite.`,
  a: `Next.js (or React Router framework mode) when SEO, fast first load, server data access, or full-stack features matter. A Vite SPA for internal tools, dashboards behind login, embedded widgets, or when you deploy to static hosting only. CRA: never for new projects.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between React Router and Reach Router for routing in React applications?`,
  k: `Two map makers who joined into one team.`,
  a: `Reach Router focused on accessibility (focus management) and relative routing. Its ideas were merged into React Router v6, and Reach Router is no longer developed. React Router v7 also merged Remix.`,
  u: `Reach Router is discontinued; use React Router.` },
{ q: `When should you use React Router instead of Reach Router, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Always use the joined team now.`,
  a: `Use React Router (v7) for all new projects. Reach Router only exists in legacy apps (e.g. old Gatsby sites) and should be migrated using React Router's migration guide.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between React Testing Library and Enzyme for testing React components?`,
  k: `Enzyme pokes inside the robot; Testing Library uses the toy the way a kid would.`,
  a: `Enzyme tested implementation details: shallow rendering, reading state, calling instance methods. React Testing Library tests behaviour through the DOM the way users interact: queries by role/label/text, user events, accessibility. Enzyme has no official adapter for React 18+ and is effectively dead.`,
  u: `Enzyme is obsolete; use RTL with Vitest or Jest.` },
{ q: `When should you use React Testing Library over Enzyme, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Always the kid-like way now.`,
  a: `RTL for all new tests and for any app on React 18/19. Enzyme only while maintaining a legacy suite on React 16/17, and plan migration (rewrite tests around user behaviour, not internals).` },
{ q: `What are the differences between styled-components and CSS Modules for styling React components?`,
  k: `styled-components writes paint inside the JavaScript; CSS Modules keeps paint in its own pot with private labels.`,
  a: `<b>styled-components</b>: runtime CSS-in-JS, styles colocated with components, dynamic styles from props, theming via context; costs runtime work and doesn't fit Server Components without 'use client'. <b>CSS Modules</b>: plain CSS files with locally scoped class names generated at build time, zero runtime, works everywhere including RSC; dynamic styles via class toggles or CSS variables.` },
{ q: `When should you use styled-components instead of CSS Modules, and vice versa?`,
  k: `New house: use the paint pots. Old house already painted with styled-components: keep it tidy.`,
  a: `CSS Modules (or Tailwind, or zero-runtime CSS-in-JS like vanilla-extract, Panda, StyleX) for new projects and anything with Server Components or strict performance budgets. styled-components when an existing codebase or design system already uses it heavily.`,
  u: `styled-components entered maintenance mode in March 2025; its maintainer advises against new adoption of runtime CSS-in-JS.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between the useContext hook and Redux for state management?`,
  k: `Listening to the loudspeaker vs asking the librarian a precise question.`,
  a: `<code>useContext</code> reads whatever value a provider supplies; any change re-renders all consumers. Redux's <code>useSelector</code> subscribes to a slice of a store and re-renders only when that selected value changes, with actions, middleware and devtools around it.` },
{ q: `When should you use useContext instead of Redux, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Small things that rarely change: loudspeaker. Lots of busy shared things: librarian.`,
  a: `useContext for theme, locale, auth session, dependency injection, or a small feature's state. Redux/Zustand for frequently updated cross-cutting client state, complex update rules, undo/redo, or when you need middleware and DevTools.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between the useRef and createRef hooks in React?`,
  k: `useRef keeps the same pocket forever; createRef sews a new pocket every time.`,
  a: `<code>useRef</code> is a hook returning the <b>same</b> ref object across renders of a function component. <code>createRef</code> is a plain function returning a <b>new</b> ref each call; used in class components (created once in the constructor or as a field). Calling createRef in a function component loses the ref every render.` },
{ q: `When should you use useRef instead of createRef, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Recipe bricks use useRef; old robots use createRef.`,
  a: `useRef in function components (always). createRef in class components, or outside React when you need a ref object factory.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between server-side rendering (SSR) and client-side rendering (CSR) in React?`,
  k: `SSR: the toy arrives built. CSR: you get a kit and build it at home.`,
  a: `<b>SSR</b> renders HTML on the server per request: faster first contentful paint, better SEO and social previews, but needs a server, adds TTFB and hydration cost. <b>CSR</b> sends an empty shell plus JS: simpler hosting and great after load, but blank until JS runs and worse for SEO. Streaming SSR and Server Components reduce SSR's downsides.` },
{ q: `When should you use server-side rendering over client-side rendering, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Show it fast to everyone (and to search engines)? SSR. A private playroom after login? CSR is fine.`,
  a: `SSR for public, content-heavy, SEO-sensitive or slow-device audiences (e-commerce, marketing, news) and personalised pages that need fast first paint. CSR for authenticated dashboards, internal tools, highly interactive apps where SEO doesn't matter. Many apps mix per route.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between static site generation (SSG) and server-side rendering (SSR) in Next.js?`,
  k: `SSG prints the book before anyone asks; SSR writes each page when someone asks.`,
  a: `<b>SSG</b> renders at build time and serves cached HTML from a CDN: fastest and cheapest, but stale until rebuild/revalidate (ISR). <b>SSR</b> renders on every request with fresh, per-user data, at the cost of server work and latency. In the App Router this is decided by data usage: <code>cookies()</code>, <code>headers()</code> or uncached fetches make a route dynamic; <code>revalidate</code> enables ISR.` },
{ q: `When should you use static site generation instead of server-side rendering, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Pages that are the same for everyone: print ahead. Pages that change per person or per minute: write fresh.`,
  a: `SSG/ISR for docs, blogs, marketing, product catalogs that change occasionally. SSR for personalised pages, search results, dashboards, and data that must be current on each request. Partial pre-rendering combines a static shell with dynamic holes.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between React.memo and useMemo for performance optimization in React?`,
  k: `React.memo guards a whole brick; useMemo remembers one answer inside a brick.`,
  a: `<code>React.memo</code> is a higher-order component that skips re-rendering a component when props are shallowly equal. <code>useMemo</code> is a hook that caches a computed value inside a component. They often work together: useMemo keeps a prop stable so React.memo can skip the child.` },
{ q: `When should you use React.memo instead of useMemo, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Brick redraws too much for no reason: React.memo. A slow sum inside the brick: useMemo.`,
  a: `React.memo for components that render often with the same props and are expensive (rows, charts). useMemo for expensive calculations or stable references inside a component. Measure first; the React Compiler can handle both automatically.` },
{ q: `What are the differences between prop drilling and useContext/useReducer for passing props in React?`,
  k: `Prop drilling: passing a note hand to hand down a long line. Context: shouting it on the loudspeaker.`,
  a: `Prop drilling passes data explicitly through each level: clear data flow and easy to trace, but noisy when many layers don't use the data. Context (often with useReducer) makes data available anywhere below the provider: less noise, but hidden dependencies and broader re-renders.` },
{ q: `When should you use prop drilling over useContext/useReducer, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Two or three hands? Just pass the note. A very long line? Use the loudspeaker.`,
  a: `Prefer props for 2–3 levels and component APIs; explicit is good. Try component composition (pass <code>children</code> or elements) before context. Use context when many distant components need the same value, and pair with useReducer when they also update it.` },
{ q: `What is the difference between React.PureComponent and functional components with React.memo?`,
  k: `The same "only redraw if changed" sign, one for robots and one for recipes.`,
  a: `Both skip renders on shallow-equal props. PureComponent also compares <b>state</b> and is for classes. <code>React.memo</code> compares only props (state changes always re-render) and accepts a custom comparison function.` },
{ q: `When should you use React.PureComponent instead of functional components with React.memo, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Robots use PureComponent; recipes use memo.`,
  a: `PureComponent only in existing class components. React.memo for function components (all new code).` },
{ q: `What are the differences between React.Fragment and the <> </> syntax in React?`,
  k: `Same invisible bag; one has a name tag pocket.`,
  a: `They're the same thing. <code>&lt;&gt;&lt;/&gt;</code> is shorthand and can't take attributes. <code>&lt;Fragment&gt;</code> can take a <code>key</code> (needed in lists).` },
{ q: `When should you use React.Fragment over the <> </> syntax, and vice versa?`,
  k: `Need a name tag? Use the long name. Otherwise the short one.`,
  a: `Use <code>&lt;&gt;</code> by default. Use <code>&lt;Fragment key={id}&gt;</code> when mapping a list where each item returns multiple elements.`,
  c: `{terms.map(t => (
  <Fragment key={t.id}><dt>{t.word}</dt><dd>{t.meaning}</dd></Fragment>
))}` },
{ q: `What is the difference between PropTypes and TypeScript for type checking in React?`,
  k: `PropTypes checked homework while the kid was reading it aloud; TypeScript checks it before class starts.`,
  a: `PropTypes were runtime checks in development, warning in the console. TypeScript checks at compile time across the whole codebase (props, state, hooks, utilities), gives editor support and refactoring safety. React 19 removed propTypes checks entirely.` },
  ],
});
